{
  "rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and, in the text field, the passage of about 250 words that comes immediately AFTER the current text in the same story.",
  "text": "Centuries bled into millennia, the concept of time losing its linear grip on Elara’s awareness. She existed in a state of perpetual now, a singular point of observation from which the universe unfolded in intricate, chaotic loops. The bird she had watched long ago had become dust, then memory, then myth, and finally, nothing at all. The stone beneath her feet had crumbled into sand, then bedrock, then magma, only to cool again into new formations that she could not name. Yet, she remained. The Threshold was her anchor, a rigid structure in the fluid chaos of existence, and she clung to it with the desperation of a drowning woman holding onto a plank of wood in a stormy sea. Occasionally, ripples in the fabric of reality would disturb her vigil. A star would collapse, sending a shockwave of gravitational distortion that she had to weave back into place, her consciousness stretching thin to patch the tear. Or a civilization would rise in the distant reaches of the galaxy, their collective hopes and fears creating a harmonic resonance that threatened to unravel the delicate balance she maintained. She would dampen their noise, not out of malice, but out of necessity. To let them scream into the void was to invite the Void to scream back. It was a terrible, silent war, fought not with swords or fire, but with the subtle manipulation of probability and causality. She was the weaver, the mender, the keeper of the seams. And in the quiet moments between the crises, when the stars burned steady and the planets turned in their predictable orbits, she would allow herself a single, fleeting thought of the girl she had been. The taste of rain on her tongue. The warmth of a hand held in the dark. These memories were fading, becoming less like experiences and more like data, but she held onto them, fragile and precious, as the only proof that she had once been human, before she became the eternal, lonely bridge."
