  "text": "The bell above the door jingled, a sharp, metallic sound that cut through the low hum of conversation. Kael looked up, his tea cup halfway to his lips. Standing in the doorway was not a merchant, nor a curious tourist, but a figure draped in the heavy, charcoal-grey cloak of the High Council. The air in the bakery seemed to tighten, the warmth retreating as if the temperature had dropped ten degrees. The woman from the North stopped mid-chew, her eyes widening. The man from the South slowly lowered his loaf, his face draining of color. Elara’s hand, which had been resting on the table, curled into a fist. \"It’s him,\" she whispered, her voice barely audible over the sudden silence that had fallen over the room. \"Lord Vane.\" Kael set his cup down with a deliberate, steady clink. He had seen Vane before, always from a distance, always behind the veil of statecraft and distant speeches. Up close, the man looked smaller than his reputation, his face a map of deep lines carved by decades of suspicion and sleepless nights. Vane did not look at the crowd. His gaze swept the room, cold and calculating, before landing squarely on Kael. He stepped inside, the door swinging shut behind him with a finality that felt like a lock engaging. The chatter died completely. Even the child outside seemed to pause, sensing the shift in the atmosphere. Vane walked to the center of the room, his boots clicking rhythmically on the stone floor. He did not sit. He simply stood, looking at the bridge through the window, then back at Kael. \"You look well, Kael,\" Vane said, his voice smooth, devoid of warmth. \"I trust the bread is to your liking?\" Kael met his gaze, refusing to look away. \"It’s warm, my lord. Just like the peace. Fragile, but warm.\" Vane’s lips twitched, not quite a smile. \"Fragile things break, Kael. And when they do, the shards cut deep. I am here to ensure the bridge does not become a grave. I have news from the North. The quarry is not empty. And the men