  "text": "Kael did not move. The silence that followed was heavier than the stone walls surrounding them. He could feel the eyes of the patrons boring into his back, a physical pressure against his spine. Elara’s breathing had become shallow, a rapid flutter of air that betrayed her fear. Kael knew he had to act, but not here. Not in the open, where Vane could use the crowd as leverage, or worse, as witnesses to a threat. He slowly pushed his chair back, the legs scraping against the stone floor with a sound that seemed deafening in the quiet. \"My lord,\" Kael said, his voice steady, though his heart hammered against his ribs like a trapped bird. \"The ovens are still hot. The bread is not yet ready. Perhaps we could continue this conversation in the back? Away from the prying eyes of those who have no business in state affairs.\" Vane’s eyes narrowed, a flicker of something dangerous passing through them. He took a step forward, closing the distance between them. The scent of old parchment and cold iron clung to him, a stark contrast to the yeast and sugar that permeated the bakery. \"You are cautious, Kael. I respect that. But caution is a luxury we no longer possess. The North is restless. The quarry men are speaking of old debts, of blood owed. If the bridge falls, it will not be a clean break. It will be a collapse. And everyone in this room, including you, will be buried under the rubble.\" He gestured to the back of the shop, a dismissive wave of his hand. \"Lead the way. And Kael? Leave the woman. She is a liability, not an ally. If she speaks, I will know. And I am not a patient man.\" Kael looked at Elara, who was staring at him with wide, terrified eyes. He gave a barely perceptible nod, a silent promise that he would protect her, even if it meant playing Vane’s game. He turned and walked toward the back, the weight of the room’s gaze following him like a chain."
